# Line endings, decided once, in the repository rather than in each machine's
# git config.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` on a Windows checkout, with nothing here to constrain it,
# produced a hundred files that `git status` called modified and `git diff`
# showed no change in: `sync-skills.mjs` wrote LF into a tree holding CRLF, and
# the difference was entirely invisible line endings. A real 44-file change was
# reported as a 130-file one.
#
# That is not a cosmetic problem. Every checker in this repo exists so that what
# changed can be seen — and a diff nobody can read is a review nobody does. It
# also left the generated trees (plugin/, .claude/skills/) looking permanently
# dirty, which is exactly the signal `build-plugin check` and CI depend on.
#
# LF EVERYWHERE, including on Windows. Everything here is Node scripts, markdown
# and JSON; every tool that touches them (node, VS Code, Cursor, PowerShell)
# reads LF without complaint, and one convention that holds on all three
# platforms beats a per-machine setting that silently differs.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Generated trees. Stated explicitly, not because the rule above would miss
# them, but because these are the files a stray CRLF hurts most: build-plugin's
# check compares the built tree against a fresh build, and a line-ending
# difference would fail it for a reason no message would explain.
plugin/**         text eol=lf
.claude/skills/** text eol=lf

# Windows-only helpers, if any are ever added, genuinely want CRLF.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf
*.ps1  text eol=crlf

# Never touched.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.rar  binary
*.pfx  binary
*.p12  binary
